            <!-- Matching order -->
            <div class="section">
                <h2 class="section-title" id="matching-order">1. The order trades are matched in</h2>
                <p>
                    Each disposal is matched against acquisitions of the same share in this order. Each step only uses the
                    shares that earlier steps left unmatched:
                </p>
                <ol>
                    <li><strong>Same day:</strong> shares bought on the same day as the disposal (TCGA 1992 s105(1)).</li>
                    <li><strong>Next 30 days:</strong> shares bought in the 30 days after the disposal, earliest first (s106A(5)).</li>
                    <li><strong>Section 104 pool:</strong> shares held before the disposal, at their average cost (s104).</li>
                    <li><strong>Later acquisitions:</strong> if all of that is not enough (a short sale), shares bought later, earliest first (s105(2)).</li>
                </ol>
                <p>
                    The app runs each step for <em>every</em> share before it moves on to the next step. For example, all
                    same-day matches are made before any 30-day match. A purchase that a same-day match uses up is no longer
                    available to an earlier disposal's 30-day match.
                </p>
                <p>
                    "The same share" means the same share identity. Trades with the same ISIN are treated as one share even
                    when the ticker differs, for example with an exchange suffix. A reused ticker with a different ISIN is
                    kept separate. You can link old and new tickers yourself, for example after a NewCo reorganisation. See the
                    <a href="https://github.com/alexpung/UK-Investment-tax-calculator/wiki/Share-identities" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">share identities wiki page</a>.
                </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Same-day rule -->
            <div class="section">
                <h2 class="section-title" id="same-day-rule">2. Same-day rule</h2>
                <p>
                    Under TCGA 1992 s105(1), all shares of the same class bought on one day count as a single purchase, and all
                    shares sold on that day count as a single sale. The app does this before any matching takes place:
                </p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Trades are combined per day and per side.</strong> Every buy of a share on one calendar date
                        becomes one combined acquisition, and every sell becomes one combined disposal. Their quantities add up,
                        and so do their costs and proceeds. Fees are included (see the <a href="HowItWorks#net-proceeds" class="link-styled">next section</a>).
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Time of day does not matter.</strong> A buy made after a sell on the same day is still matched
                        against it. The date used is the trade date in your broker statement. The app does not convert
                        between time zones.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Matching is by quantity.</strong> The combined disposal is matched against the combined
                        acquisition up to whichever quantity is smaller.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Leftover shares carry on.</strong> If more was sold than bought, the rest of the sale goes to the
                        30-day rule and then the Section 104 pool. If more was bought than sold, the extra shares can still be
                        matched with a disposal in the previous 30 days. Anything left after that goes into the pool.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>One combined trade, one disposal.</strong> On the Disposal Summary, a day's sells appear as a
                        single disposal. The individual broker trades are on the Imported Trades page. The exported
                        calculation file lists them under each disposal.
                    </li>
                </ul>

                <div class="card mb-3">
                    <div class="card-body">
                        <h3 class="h5 card-title">Worked example: two sells and a buy on the same day</h3>
                        <p>Before 10 June 2024 you hold 100 shares in the Section 104 pool, with a total cost of £1,000. On 10 June 2024:</p>
                        <div class="table-responsive">
                            <table class="table table-sm">
                                <thead>
                                    <tr><th>Time</th><th>Trade</th><th class="text-end">Gross</th><th class="text-end">Fee</th><th class="text-end">Used by the app</th></tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr><td>09:00</td><td>Sell 40</td><td class="text-end">£600</td><td class="text-end">£5</td><td class="text-end">£595 net proceeds</td></tr>
                                    <tr><td>11:00</td><td>Buy 50</td><td class="text-end">£700</td><td class="text-end">£10</td><td class="text-end">£710 cost</td></tr>
                                    <tr><td>15:00</td><td>Sell 40</td><td class="text-end">£600</td><td class="text-end">£5</td><td class="text-end">£595 net proceeds</td></tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            The app combines the two sells into <strong>one disposal of 80 shares for £1,190</strong> net. The buy
                            becomes one acquisition of 50 shares costing £710. The buy came after the first sell, but that makes no
                            difference.
                        </p>
                        <div class="table-responsive">
                            <table class="table table-sm">
                                <thead>
                                    <tr><th>Match</th><th class="text-end">Shares</th><th class="text-end">Proceeds</th><th class="text-end">Allowable cost</th><th class="text-end">Gain</th></tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr><td>Same day</td><td class="text-end">50</td><td class="text-end">£1,190 × 50/80 = £743.75</td><td class="text-end">£710.00</td><td class="text-end">£33.75</td></tr>
                                    <tr><td>Section 104</td><td class="text-end">30</td><td class="text-end">£1,190 × 30/80 = £446.25</td><td class="text-end">£1,000 × 30/100 = £300.00</td><td class="text-end">£146.25</td></tr>
                                    <tr class="fw-bold"><td>Total</td><td class="text-end">80</td><td class="text-end">£1,190.00</td><td class="text-end">£1,010.00</td><td class="text-end">£180.00</td></tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                        </div>
                        <p class="mb-0">
                            Afterwards the pool holds 70 shares costing £700. The same-day purchase never enters the pool, because
                            the same-day match uses all of it.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Net proceeds -->
            <div class="section">
                <h2 class="section-title" id="net-proceeds">3. Net proceeds instead of gross proceeds</h2>
                <p>
                    Dealing costs are allowable, whether they are paid on a purchase or on a sale. The app puts them on the side
                    of the trade they belong to, so that every trade carries a single net figure:
                </p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Acquisitions:</strong> cost = price paid <strong>+</strong> commission, stamp duty and other fees.</li>
                    <li><strong>Disposals:</strong> proceeds = amount received <strong>−</strong> commission and other fees, i.e. <strong>net proceeds</strong>.</li>
                </ul>
                <p>
                    Where the fees come from depends on the broker. For Interactive Brokers statements, commission and taxes
                    are read separately and converted to pounds at the exchange rate given in the statement. For FreeTrade,
                    Trading 212 and Interactive Investor exports, the app uses the broker's total amount in pounds, which already
                    includes the dealing charges. Some trades have later adjustments, such as an option premium that is added
                    to the shares it was exercised into. Those adjustments are applied to the same net figure.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Where a trade is split across matches, its fees are split too, in proportion to quantity.</strong>
                    Matches use the trade's net figure × (shares matched ÷ total shares). In the example above, the £10 of selling
                    fees is split £6.25 to the same-day match (50/80) and £3.75 to the Section 104 match (30/80). Because a day's
                    trades are combined first, fees from all of that day's sells are added together before they are split. You
                    cannot assign a fee to one particular match.
                </p>
                <p>
                    So the <strong>Disposal Proceeds</strong> in the CGT Yearly Summary are <em>net</em> of selling fees, and the
                    <strong>Allowable Costs</strong> do <em>not</em> include selling fees again. The tax return only accepts whole pounds, so
                    the summary rounds in your favour: proceeds and gains down, allowable costs and losses up. HMRC's computation working sheet in the
                    <a href="https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/self-assessment-capital-gains-summary-sa108" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SA108 notes</a>
                    works the same way. It subtracts the incidental costs of disposal from the proceeds to give "net disposal
                    proceeds", and the total costs include only the cost of acquisition.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Some calculators and guides report gross proceeds instead and add the selling fees to allowable costs, for
                    example <a href="https://cgtvisualiser.co.uk/guides/how-to-calculate-capital-gains-tax-on-shares/" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CGT Visualiser</a>.
                    The gain is the same either way. Only the split between "proceeds" and "costs" moves by the amount of
                    the selling fees:
                </p>
                <div class="table-responsive">
                    <table class="table table-sm">
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Same-day example</th><th class="text-end">Proceeds</th><th class="text-end">Allowable costs</th><th class="text-end">Gain</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>This app (net proceeds)</td><td class="text-end">£1,190</td><td class="text-end">£1,010</td><td class="text-end">£180</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Gross proceeds, selling fees in costs</td><td class="text-end">£1,200</td><td class="text-end">£1,020</td><td class="text-end">£180</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="alert alert-info alert-custom" role="note">
                    If you compare this app with a calculator that uses gross proceeds, expect the proceeds and costs to differ
                    by the total selling fees while the gains agree. If you would rather enter gross proceeds on your return,
                    add the selling fees back to both figures. The Imported Trades page shows the gross and net amount of each
                    trade.
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- 30-day rule -->
            <div class="section">
                <h2 class="section-title" id="thirty-day-rule">4. 30-day (bed and breakfast) rule</h2>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Window:</strong> acquisitions from 1 to 30 days after the disposal date, counted in calendar
                        days. Acquisitions on the disposal day itself are handled by the same-day rule first.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Order:</strong> the earliest acquisition in the window is matched first (TCGA 1992 s106A(5)(b)).
                        If several disposals could use the same later purchase, the earlier disposal goes first (s106A(4)).
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Non-residents:</strong> an acquisition made on or after 22 March 2006 while you are not UK
                        resident is not matched under this rule (s106A(5A), see
                        <a href="https://www.gov.uk/hmrc-internal-manuals/capital-gains-manual/cg51560" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CG51560</a>).
                        The app uses the periods you set on the Residency Status page. The rule also covers acquisitions made
                        while you are "Treaty non-resident", but the app has no separate setting for that.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Corporate actions in the window:</strong> if a stock split or similar event falls between the
                        disposal and the repurchase, the matched quantity is adjusted for it.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Only what you import is seen:</strong> purchases inside an ISA or pension should not be in your
                        imported files, so they are never matched. Do include any trades in the 30 days after the tax year ends,
                        because they can change that year's result.
                    </li>
                </ul>
                <div class="card mb-3">
                    <div class="card-body">
                        <h3 class="h5 card-title">Worked example</h3>
                        <p>
                            You bought 1,000 shares in May 2024 for £4,990 plus a £10 fee, so the pool holds 1,000 shares costing
                            £5,000. On 3 March 2025 you sell all 1,000 for £4,000 less a £10 fee (net £3,990). On 20 March 2025
                            you buy 1,000 back for £4,100 plus a £10 fee (£4,110).
                        </p>
                        <p class="mb-0">
                            The sale is matched with the March repurchase, not with the pool: £3,990 − £4,110 = a
                            <strong>loss of £120</strong>. The pool keeps the original 1,000 shares at £5,000.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Section 104 -->
            <div class="section">
                <h2 class="section-title" id="section-104">5. Section 104 pool</h2>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        Shares that the same-day and 30-day rules do not use go into one pool per share. The pool's cost includes
                        purchase fees, so it is an average cost.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        A disposal matched with the pool takes cost = pool cost × shares sold ÷ shares in the pool, so the
                        average cost per share does not change.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        The app works through the pool in date order. Corporate actions that change the pool, such as stock
                        splits, takeovers, spin-offs, return of capital and excess reportable income adjustments, are applied
                        on their dates, between the trades.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        Every change to the pool is recorded, so you can follow the running quantity and cost on the
                        Section 104 Data page.
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- Short sales -->
            <div class="section">
                <h2 class="section-title" id="short-sales">6. Shares sold before they are bought</h2>
                <p>
                    If a disposal is still not fully matched after the pool (you sold more than you held), the rest waits. It is
                    matched with later acquisitions, earliest first, and these matches are labelled "Cover short selling".
                    Until then, the Disposal Summary shows how many shares are still unmatched. Options and futures follow the same matching
                    steps, with some extra rules described in the
                    <a href="https://github.com/alexpung/UK-Investment-tax-calculator/wiki/Traded-Financial-Options" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">options</a> and
                    <a href="https://github.com/alexpung/UK-Investment-tax-calculator/wiki/Future-Contracts" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">futures</a> wiki pages.
                </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Check workings -->
            <div class="section">
                <h2 class="section-title" id="check-workings">7. Checking the workings in the app</h2>
                <p>After you run a calculation, these pages show each step described above:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><a href="TradeMatchPage" class="link-styled">Trade Matches</a> lists every match with its type (same day, bed and breakfast, Section 104, cover short selling), its quantities, proceeds and allowable cost.</li>
                    <li><a href="CalculationViewPage" class="link-styled">Disposal Summary</a> shows each combined trade, how many of its shares each rule matched, and its proceeds, allowable cost and gain.</li>
                    <li><a href="ImportedTradesDataPage" class="link-styled">Imported Trades</a> shows each broker trade with its gross and net amount.</li>
                    <li><a href="Section104DataPage" class="link-styled">Section 104 Data</a> shows the pool's history for each share.</li>
                    <li><a href="CgtYearlyTaxSummaryPage" class="link-styled">CGT Yearly Summary</a> gives the totals for your tax return, using net proceeds.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="alert alert-warning alert-custom" role="alert">
                <strong>Not tax advice.</strong> This page describes how the software calculates its figures. It is not tax,
                legal or financial advice. The app does not handle every exception in tax law. You are responsible for
                checking what you report.
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
